[Music]  in Europe in the sixteenth century most  people drank beer instead of water the  brewing process killed all of those  nasty microbes that were found in your  average 16th century cup of water so  beer was actually the cheapest safe  beverage available when coffee and tea  became widely and cheaply available in  Europe thanks to the opening of trade  routes with Latin America and East Asia  in the 17th century they offered a new  safe alternative to water and the  subsequent transition from drinking  mostly alcohol to drinking mostly  caffeine coincided with the most  technologically productive period in the  continents history the Industrial  Revolution I love this correlation  because it illustrates everything that I  love about history history pushes us to  be curious about cause and effect as a  kid I assumed that these brilliant  scientists and inventors that I had  learned about who invented the steam  engine who learned how to harness coal  and produce electricity were solely  responsible for our being ushered into  the modern era but studying history real  history messy history shows a very  different story where the Industrial  Revolution was dependent not only on  these brilliant inventors and scientists  but also on Europe's transition from  being permanently drunk to permanently  caffeinated history shows us how small  changes in the way that we live our  lives can trigger massive societal  transformation historians study the past  to uncover the unintended consequences  of our actions we're not always  encouraged to think like historians here  at the GSB in fact I think most of us  would probably consider ourselves  students of the future after all this  place is dedicated to the things that  haven't happened yet and the people who  are about to dream them up but I think  that we all should think  like historians see I think that Silicon  Valley needs to learn how to think like  historians here we only consider the  past for long enough to figure out how  to disrupt it we're not often  preoccupied with unintended consequences  and so many of the innovations that  we've built are actually causing bigger  problems than the ones that they set out  to solve in the first place  to better understand the impact of our  innovation we need a better framework  for mapping out cause and effect we need  to understand the impacts of our actions  for example the National Safety Council  realized that car accidents increased by  12 point three percent over a five year  period due to an increase in distracted  driving which is really really just a  euphemism for not being able to put your  smart phone down or traffic congestion  in San Francisco it's gotten worse right  it's increased by sixty percent over the  last ten years and more than half of  that is attributed to Oberer and lyft  and Airbnb is accused of decreasing  long-term housing stock in cities that  are already facing housing shortages and  affordability issues so how can we learn  to think like historians here in Silicon  Valley to minimize risk and to mitigate  some of this damage well thinking like a  historian means looking closely at what  we're leaving behind first of all  historian spends a lot of time digging  through primary source records and  artifacts which can be broadly  classified into two different categories  trash and triumph trash is everything  that the people in the past meant to  throw away triumph is everything that  they intentionally left behind for us to  find and both are equally important in  understanding what life was actually  like in the past and historians have  learned just as much from ossified food  remains as they have from the  hieroglyphs that are carved in the walls  of the ancient pyramids when it comes to  understanding what it was like to live  in ancient Egypt now I would argue that  we  spend too much time focused on the  triumph and not enough time looking at  the trash when it comes to understanding  our impact here now sure the triumph has  an interesting story to tell about our  priorities as a civilization and a  society just as the ancient Egyptians  built pyramids to house their dead  leader gods Jeff Bezos  is building a 42 million-dollar clock in  the desert as a testament to technology  that will continue to function for  millennia sure interesting story but the  trash has a really important story to  tell to the trash tells a story about  how our lives are changing in the most  mundane but often most fundamental ways  historians have learned so much about  who was trading with whom  by studying discarded pottery shards and  tracking them through ancient trash  dumps and we too can learn about the  behaviors and patterns that our  innovations are encouraging by studying  the things that people did not intend to  leave behind what does the refuse in a  rideshare drivers trunk say about the  amount of time that he has to spend in  his car what do the deleted direct  messages in a teenage girl's Instagram  account say about the experience of  being a young woman online in addition  to looking at what we're leaving behind  we also have to understand whom we're  leaving out we need to ask questions  about whose voices are missing from the  stories that we hear of our impacts  historians know all too well that there  is no such thing as a reliable narrator  everybody's explanation of the facts is  tinged by their own unique perspective a  horrifying bloodbath and a justified  seizure of power are both valid  descriptions of the French Revolution  depending on where you're standing in  1789 the historians job is to piece  together these multiple perspectives to  get a comprehensive view of what  happened and why when it comes to  building technology and developing new  ideas  it's easy to lose sight of that big  picture view and get trapped in our own  perspectives just look at the myriad  tools that we've built to solve problems  that are only relevant to the urban tech  elite think an uber for dog walking app  that's currently valued at 300 million  dollars  seems a little unnecessary we need to  ask questions about whose voices are  missing from our understanding of the  worlds we need to be more curious as  businesspeople and technologists what  would happen if we stepped out of the  view of our direct customers and  interviewed people who are impacted by  the second degree effects of emerging  technology what might we learn there's a  sort of historical hubris here in  Silicon Valley there's a sense that the  things that we're building are going to  change the world and we know exactly how  and why whenever I hear a tech CEO or a  start-up founder of venture capitalists  talk about how their company is going to  change the world I can't help but think  about my favorite little nugget of  history see the introduction of caffeine  to the European populace may have helped  to spur the Industrial Revolution but  there's no way that any conquistador or  merchant could have possibly known that  as they carted bushels of tea leaves and  coffee beans across oceans and deserts  the introduction of new goods to the  European market could have very easily  pushed history in a different direction  highly relevant to today the bubonic  plague which killed every third year a  peon in the middle of the 14th century  was also a side-effect of open trade  small changes can trigger destruction  just as easily as they trigger  transformation and innovation we need to  better understand our impact and to the  questions that historians ask to study  the past can help us do this Isaac  Asimov once said that great science  fiction writers don't imagine the car  they imagine the traffic I sure wish  that uber had imagined the traffic when  they built their app we all need to get  better at imagining and fixing the  traffic that our innovations builds and  the study of history can help us ask the  right questions so that we're building a  future that we all actually want to live  in thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

